// Layer 2 — domain extractor facts.
//
// GENERATED by `mix argus.gen.dl` from Argus.Schema.layer_2/0 — do not edit.
// Schema version 66.
//
// Include this instead of declaring fact relations by hand. Souffle
// prunes input relations no rule reads, so including the whole layer
// costs nothing in the solve.

// GenServer.cast target detected in code.
.decl async_cast(caller_func: symbol, callee_mod: symbol)
.input async_cast

// Try/catch handler that catches all exceptions without filtering or reraising.
.decl bare_rescue(id: symbol, func: symbol, guard_end: symbol)
.input bare_rescue

// Resolved argument value at a call site. Enables interprocedural constant propagation: `resolved_arg` in `clientlib/calls.dl` traces literal values from call sites through forwarding chains to resolve `sync_call`/`async_cast` targets the extractors couldn't resolve statically. Forwarded parameters are NOT values here — they are their own relation, `call_arg_forward`.
//
.decl call_arg(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, value: symbol)
.input call_arg

// At some call to the callee, the argument depends on the source: made from it, or computed under a test on it. Unlike call_arg_derived, control counts, and the sources include call results. Function-level.
//
.decl call_arg_depends(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input call_arg_depends

// At some call site in the caller, the argument is data-dependent on one of the caller's parameters: destructured out of it, built into a tuple or a binary with it, or returned by a call known to hand its argument's data through. A superset of call_arg_forward (identity is a dependence). A closure built with make_fun3 counts as a call whose trailing parameters are the captured environment. Function-level, so a body edit that keeps the flow does not move it.
//
.decl call_arg_derived(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, param_pos: number)
.input call_arg_derived

// The argument at `arg_pos` is element `index` of the caller's own parameter (`elem(record, 1)`, a `get_tuple_element` or the `element/2` BIF): what a helper that names the value by its own parameter is handed, in the caller's terms. Only emitted where call_arg says 'dynamic'.
//
.decl call_arg_element(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, param_pos: number, index: number)
.input call_arg_element

// The argument at `arg_pos` was read from a map under a literal key in the caller (`start_timer(ms, state.ref)`): which piece of the caller's state a helper is handed. Only emitted where call_arg says 'dynamic'.
//
.decl call_arg_field(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, key: symbol)
.input call_arg_field

// A call site passes one of the caller's own parameters straight through as an argument — the forwarding step interprocedural constant propagation walks backwards.
//
// Split out of `call_arg`, where it used to be encoded in the value column as the string `"arg:N"` and decoded in Datalog with `to_number(substr(...))`. `to_number` is a PARTIAL functor: it aborts on input that is not numeric. The guard that kept non-forwarding values away from it was a sibling conjunct, and Souffle does not promise conjunct order — the default schedule happened to be safe, but the magic-set transform reordered and aborted with `to_number("mic")`. A structured column cannot be scheduled into a crash.
//
.decl call_arg_forward(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, fwd_pos: number)
.input call_arg_forward

// call_arg_depends by data alone, for calls (not closures): the argument is made from the source. Function-level.
//
.decl call_arg_reads(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input call_arg_reads

// The argument at `arg_pos` is a tuple the caller builds, and its element `index` is `(source, value)` in the caller's terms: a record's table (element 0) and key (element 1), an ETS object's key. A callee that names the element by its parameter is handed this. Elements the caller cannot name are not emitted.
//
.decl call_arg_tuple(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, index: number, source: symbol, value: symbol)
.input call_arg_tuple

// Some call to the callee runs only because of a test on the source: everything the callee does is decided by it. Building a closure counts as a call to it. Function-level.
//
.decl call_decided(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input call_decided

// A call to a process or OTP API, or to anything that starts a process, with what became of its result and whether the site sits inside a try, and what it acts on when a literal says. One row per site, so a rule can count how the other sites of the same callee (on the same target) behave and report the one that disagrees.
//
.decl call_result(id: symbol, func: symbol, callee: symbol, fate: symbol, guard: symbol, guard_end: symbol, target: symbol)
.input call_result

// The callback's catch-all does nothing with the message but log it or ignore it: every path from its body hands the message, or what is made of it, only to Logger, `:logger`, IO or `inspect/2`. GenServer's own handle_info/2 is one. A catch-all that calls anything else with it, returns it, stores it or tests it is not.
//
.decl callback_drops(func: symbol, callback: symbol)
.input callback_drops

// A `{:noreply, _}` return site in handle_call/3 that some execution reaches having never read `from`. Per site rather than per function, because handle_call compiles every clause into one function and a sibling clause that defers correctly would otherwise vouch for one that does not.
//
// Established by walking the intra-function block graph from the entry across blocks that do not read `from`. `from` arrives in {x,1}, and a read is either a mention of that register or a call of arity two or more, since calls take arguments positionally and a body passing `from` straight through compiles to no move at all.
//
.decl callback_drops_from(id: symbol, func: symbol)
.input callback_drops_from

// Some clause other than a catch-all takes the message by its shape alone, never comparing it or its tag to a value: `msg when is_atom(msg)` (`any`), `{ref, result} when is_reference(ref)` (`tuple`, a tuple of any tag). `callback_tag` names nothing such a clause takes, so a rule asking whether a message is taken asks this too.
//
.decl callback_open(func: symbol, callback: symbol, shape: symbol)
.input callback_open

// The callback has a clause whose message is a tuple headed by a reference — `{ref, result} when is_reference(ref)`, the reply of a task started with async_nolink.
//
.decl callback_ref_head(func: symbol, callback: symbol)
.input callback_ref_head

// A literal return tag of an OTP callback — the first element of a tuple built into {x,0} immediately before `return`. Absent when the callback tail-calls, since the shape then belongs to the callee; consumers must treat absence as unknown rather than as "returns nothing".
//
.decl callback_return(id: symbol, func: symbol, callback: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input callback_return

// The reason of a `{:stop, reason, ...}` return from an OTP callback, when it is a literal atom or a `{:shutdown, term}` literal. Absent when the reason is computed, so consumers treat absence as unknown.
//
.decl callback_stop_reason(id: symbol, func: symbol, reason: symbol)
.input callback_stop_reason

// A message tag a callback matches. Over-approximated: every atom compared anywhere in the body counts, without tracking registers. Consumers ask whether a tag is NOT handled, so over-approximating suppresses findings rather than inventing them.
//
.decl callback_tag(func: symbol, callback: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input callback_tag

// A literal integer timeout in an OTP callback's return — the third element of `{:ok, state, ms}` or `{:noreply, state, ms}`, the fourth of `{:reply, reply, state, ms}`. The message it schedules, `:timeout`, is cancelled by any other message arriving first.
//
.decl callback_timeout(id: symbol, func: symbol, callback: symbol, timeout_ms: number)
.input callback_timeout

// The callback has a catch-all clause, so no tag can fail to match. Established from the bytecode: a multi-clause function raises by jumping to its own `func_info` label, so nothing branching there means every input matches. A guarded catch-all is correctly NOT total.
//
.decl callback_total(func: symbol, callback: symbol)
.input callback_total

// A cancel_timer inside a `handle_info/2` clause whose head is a literal message: `def handle_info(:heartbeat, s)` — the head test on the first argument dominates the cancel, and nothing before it overwrote the argument. Cancelling the timer whose message this clause is handling cancels a timer that has already fired.
//
.decl cancel_clause(id: symbol, func: symbol, message: symbol)
.input cancel_clause

// A `case` inside the handler, reached after comparing `tag`, has no clause for some value: a reason the handler did not anticipate is a CaseClauseError rather than a result. The tag identifies the case — the compiler emits a clause-less case of its own for `e.field`.
//
.decl catch_falls_through(id: symbol, func: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input catch_falls_through

// A reason tag a clause discriminates on, attributed to the class that clause catches. Within a clause every compared atom counts (a `case` in the body too), the over-approximation `callback_tag` makes: rules ask whether a tag is NOT handled, so seeing too many suppresses findings rather than inventing them.
//
.decl catch_tag(id: symbol, func: symbol, class: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input catch_tag

// Some clause of the handler catches the class outright — `catch :exit, reason ->`, `rescue e ->` — so no reason of that class escapes it.
//
.decl catch_total(id: symbol, func: symbol, class: symbol)
.input catch_total

// The restart type a module's own child_spec/1 gives a shorthand {Mod, args} spec.
.decl child_spec_restart(mod: symbol, restart: symbol)
.input child_spec_restart

// The call at `id` runs only while `func`'s first argument is `tag` — the atom, or the first element of the tuple, some path to the call tested it against — one row per such tag. A call some path reaches without establishing a tag has no row. Exact per path, unlike `callback_tag`: a synchronous call chain follows the clause of handle_call/3 a request enters, and the clause of a guarded dispatcher (`route(:local, n)`) a literal argument enters, so the `:echo` clause that closes a cycle does not stand for the `:answer` clause beside it.
//
.decl clause_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input clause_call

// Dynamic code execution or OS command call.
.decl code_execution(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol)
.input code_execution

// A call that claims a name or starts a process: a registration, a named start, a via-registered start, or start_child, whose name hides in the child spec.
//
.decl creating_op(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, scope: symbol, source: symbol, key: symbol)
.input creating_op

// A `Process.demonitor/1,2`. `flush` records whether `[:flush]` was passed, which is the difference between cancelling a future message and removing one already in the mailbox. Unreadable options are recorded as `no_flush`, the direction that keeps a finding rather than discharging one on a guess.
//
.decl demonitor_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, flush: symbol)
.input demonitor_call

// Mnesia or DETS distributed store operation.
.decl distributed_store_op(id: symbol, func: symbol, store: symbol, op: symbol)
.input distributed_store_op

// Runtime-spawned child via `DynamicSupervisor.start_child/2`. Captured so analyses like `one_for_one_coupling` can see workers added at runtime (connection pools, per-tenant supervisors, plugin systems) that wouldn't appear in any static `init/1` child spec scan.
//
.decl dynamic_child(sup: symbol, child_mod: symbol, caller_func: symbol)
.input dynamic_child

// What identifies an argument of the call, in ets_key's vocabulary: a callee's insert keyed by its parameter is keyed, in the caller, by what the caller passes there.
//
.decl ets_call_arg(id: symbol, callee: symbol, pos: symbol, source: symbol, value: symbol)
.input ets_call_arg

// Two effects of one function in order within one trip through it: inserts, and the calls a callee's inserts hide behind. An edge into a block that dominates its source is a loop's back edge and is not followed, so a loop body's writes are ordered as each iteration makes them, not both ways. At least one of the two is an insert or a call to a function of the same module that inserts.
//
.decl ets_effect_order(func: symbol, first: symbol, then: symbol)
.input ets_effect_order

// What identifies the key operand of an ETS operation; for insert and insert_new, the first element of the object. Two operations agreeing on source and key touch the same row.
//
.decl ets_key(id: symbol, source: symbol, key: symbol)
.input ets_key

// ETS table creation point.
.decl ets_new(id: symbol, func: symbol, name: symbol)
.input ets_new

// ETS read/write/delete operation.
.decl ets_op(id: symbol, func: symbol, table_ref: symbol, op: symbol, kind: symbol)
.input ets_op

// The table operand of an ETS operation is the function's own parameter.
.decl ets_op_param(id: symbol, pos: number)
.input ets_op_param

// Parsed option from :ets.new/2.
.decl ets_option(id: symbol, key: symbol, value: symbol)
.input ets_option

// Where the table operand of an ETS operation was read from: a root and the map keys read from it (Resolve.access_path/4). Two tables handed to a function in one map, `%{forward: f, reverse: r}`, are two paths under one root where ets_op knows both by the name they were created with. Absent when the operand's writers disagree; a table named by a join (`cfg.table || @default`) is to have one row per arm.
//
.decl ets_table_path(id: symbol, source: symbol, root: symbol, path: symbol)
.input ets_table_path

// At some call in the caller, or in the environment of a closure it builds, the argument is the table reference :ets.new/2 returned in the caller. An unnamed table is known by the name it was created with, so an operation on the parameter joins ets_new like a named one. Function-level, like call_arg.
//
.decl ets_tid_arg(caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: number, name: symbol)
.input ets_tid_arg

// What identifies an element past the key of the object an insert or insert_new writes, in ets_key's vocabulary: a row holding a value another table is keyed by joins that table's key on it. An element nothing identifies has no row.
//
.decl ets_value(id: symbol, pos: number, source: symbol, value: symbol)
.input ets_value

// Explicit Process.exit/2 or :erlang.exit/1,2 call.
.decl exit_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, target: symbol)
.input exit_call

// `:global` synchronization primitives. The retries field is the third argument of `:global.set_lock/3` (or `:global.trans/4`); analyses use it to distinguish blocking calls (`infinity` or large positive integers) from non-blocking try-once calls (`0`).
//
// `:global.set_lock/2` and `:global.trans/2,3` default to infinity retries — recorded as `"infinity"` even when the source code omits the argument.
//
.decl global_op(id: symbol, func: symbol, op: symbol, retries: symbol)
.input global_op

// `:global.register_name` call. Arity distinguishes the race-prone default (`/2`) from a call that supplies its own conflict-resolution function (`/3`).
//
.decl global_register(id: symbol, func: symbol, name: symbol, arity: symbol)
.input global_register

// A `handle_continue(tag, _)` clause defined by a module. The
// deferred-startup-deadlock analysis pairs this with `init_continues_to`
// to find handle_continue bodies reachable from a module's init.
//
.decl handle_continue_clause(mod: symbol, tag: symbol, func_id: symbol)
.input handle_continue_clause

// A route from `Phoenix.Router.__routes__/0`. `pipe_through` is absent because Phoenix compiles pipelines into the dispatch function rather than into this literal, so whether a route is authenticated is derivable but not from here.
//
.decl http_route(router: symbol, verb: symbol, path: symbol, plug: symbol, action: symbol)
.input http_route

// Call to function returning tagged tuple where result is not pattern matched.
.decl ignored_error_result(id: symbol, func: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input ignored_error_result

// Module implements a specific OTP behaviour.
.decl implements_behaviour(mod: symbol, behaviour: symbol)
.input implements_behaviour

// Tracks every fallback to a "dynamic" placeholder or an outright skipped fact emission. Populated only when imprecision tracing is enabled for the current pipeline run — the `coverage` analysis turns it on, every other analysis runs with tracing off and produces no rows in this relation.
//
// The `category` vocabulary is documented in `lib/argus/extractor/helpers.ex` and is treated as a versioned API: changes are noted in CHANGELOG so coverage diff tooling can keep stable keys.
//
.decl imprecision(category: symbol, func: symbol, relation: symbol, reason: symbol)
.input imprecision

// A call with a known observable effect, classified by `Argus.Purity.Effects`. The category says WHAT the effect is; the mode says whether it changes anything.
//
// Both dimensions are needed because contracts differ in what they forbid. Purity rejects reads and writes alike — `Application.get_env/2` already breaks referential transparency. A transaction body only cares about writes: a config read has nothing to roll back, while an HTTP POST has already left the machine. One dimension would force every config read to be reported as a transaction hazard.
//
.decl impure_call(id: symbol, caller: symbol, api: symbol, category: symbol, mode: symbol)
.input impure_call

// Records that a GenServer module returns `{:ok, _, {:continue, tag}}` from
// `init/1` or `{:noreply, _, {:continue, tag}}` from any handler. The
// deferred-startup-deadlock analysis uses this to identify modules whose
// `handle_continue/2` clauses run during the startup phase.
//
.decl init_continues_to(mod: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input init_continues_to

// A function another module's macro wrote into this one — `use Ecto.Repo` defines `stop/1` in the repo — read from the `context:` Elixir records in each definition's debug-info metadata, or its `generated: true` marker (`generated`). Its call sites are the library's, not the program's. Erlang modules yield no rows.
//
.decl macro_generated(func: symbol, by: symbol)
.input macro_generated

// A call after which something other than a peer's request can land in the calling process's mailbox: a Task.async reply, a timer message, a subscription's broadcasts, a message the function sends to itself, or caller-supplied code run through a closure or apply. What makes a partial handle_info/2 a risk rather than a style note.
//
.decl mailbox_writer(id: symbol, func: symbol, kind: symbol)
.input mailbox_writer

// The function handles (part of) a :DOWN message: an argument register, or one projected from it, is compared to :DOWN in a clause head. Emitted for every function rather than only named callbacks, because a gen_statem funnels :info events through private helpers.
//
.decl matches_down(func: symbol)
.input matches_down

// A Mnesia dirty operation, outside any transaction's serialization, with the table and key it touches. The one-argument forms carry both in a tuple: {table, key}, or a record whose first element is its table and whose second is its key.
//
.decl mnesia_op(id: symbol, func: symbol, op: symbol, kind: symbol, table_source: symbol, table: symbol, key_source: symbol, key: symbol)
.input mnesia_op

// A `Process.monitor/1` or `:erlang.monitor/2`. Once it returns, a `{:DOWN, ref, :process, object, reason}` arrives unless cancelled — so a process that monitors is a process that receives `:DOWN`.
//
.decl monitor_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, target: symbol)
.input monitor_call

// The reference `Process.monitor/1` returned at this site is discarded: the next thing to happen to the result register is a write that does not read it. Nothing can ever demonitor this monitor; it ends only when the monitored process does.
//
.decl monitor_ref_dropped(id: symbol, func: symbol)
.input monitor_ref_dropped

// A process name looked up — Process.whereis/1, :erlang.whereis/1, Registry.lookup/2 — with what identifies the name, so a creating op on the same name can be joined to it, and whether the result is tested against nil (or []) before use. Process.registered/0 and :erlang.registered/0 look up every name at once: source any.
//
.decl name_lookup(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, scope: symbol, source: symbol, key: symbol, checked: symbol)
.input name_lookup

// A registered name given up — Process.unregister/1, :erlang.unregister/1 — which raises when the name is no longer registered: a lookup that decides it is stale once the process exits or another process unregisters first.
//
.decl name_release(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, source: symbol, key: symbol)
.input name_release

// Named process registration detected in code.
.decl named_process(mod: symbol, name: symbol)
.input named_process

// Node or :net_kernel operation.
.decl node_operation(id: symbol, func: symbol, op: symbol)
.input node_operation

// At `id`, `callee`'s parameter `arg_pos` may hold the source: a call into project code (`call`), a server start's init argument (`Mod:init/1`, `init`), a spawned function's arguments (`spawn`), a child spec's argument (`Mod:start_link/1`, `child`) or a closure's captured variables, its trailing parameters (`closure`). OTP's and Elixir's own modules are not followed.
//
.decl pid_arg(id: symbol, caller: symbol, callee: symbol, arg_pos: symbol, via: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_arg

// `obj` updates the source: the fields `obj` does not set (pid_sets) are the source's. A cons cell's base is its tail.
//
.decl pid_base(func: symbol, obj: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_base

// The GenServer-style call or cast at `id`, or the send (info), may target the source or a literal name: what resolves a sync_call recorded as "dynamic".
//
.decl pid_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, api_kind: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_call

// The field `sel` of `obj` may hold the source.
.decl pid_field(func: symbol, obj: symbol, sel: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_field

// The load `load` (a `load` source in `func`) reads the field `sel` of the source: `state.conn`, `elem(msg, 1)`, a clause head's `{:subscribe, pid}`, `Map.get(state, :conn)`.
//
.decl pid_load(func: symbol, load: symbol, sel: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_load

// The message of the call, cast or send at `id` may be the source. It reaches the handler of the server the pid_call rows of the same site resolve to (handle_call/3, handle_cast/2, handle_info/2) as its message parameter: how a subscriber's pid gets into a server's state.
//
.decl pid_message(id: symbol, func: symbol, api_kind: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_message

// A term that holds a source, named by the instruction that built it (put_map_*, put_tuple2, put_list, update_record, or a call whose result has a known shape: a start's `{:ok, pid}`, `Map.put/3`).
//
.decl pid_object(func: symbol, obj: symbol, shape: symbol, tag: symbol, arity: symbol)
.input pid_object

// The call at `id` registers the source under `name` (Process.register/2, :erlang.register/2).
.decl pid_register(id: symbol, func: symbol, name: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_register

// The project call at `id`, whose result is a `result` source (src = `id`).
.decl pid_result(id: symbol, func: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input pid_result

// `func` may return the source, directly or by a tail call.
.decl pid_return(func: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_return

// The send at `id` (`send/2`, `!`, Process.send/3) goes to the source or, with src_kind `name`, to a literal name. Keyed on the site because the finding about what it sends anchors there.
//
.decl pid_send(id: symbol, func: symbol, message: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_send

// The update `obj` sets `sel`, shadowing its base's field.
.decl pid_sets(obj: symbol, sel: symbol)
.input pid_sets

// The exit signal (Process.exit/2, :erlang.exit/2), monitor (Process.monitor/1,2, :erlang.monitor/2,3), link or unlink at `id` goes to the source.
//
.decl pid_signal(id: symbol, func: symbol, signal: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input pid_signal

// Port creation point. A port is owned by the opening process and dies when it terminates — so, like an ETS table, it attributes to that process in the supervision tree.
.decl port_open(id: symbol, func: symbol, mechanism: symbol, target: symbol)
.input port_open

// A call made after Supervisor.start_link returned, in the same function.
.decl post_start_call(func: symbol, site: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input post_start_call

// Process link between modules.
.decl process_link(from_mod: symbol, to_mod: symbol)
.input process_link

// Process name registration.
.decl process_register(id: symbol, func: symbol, name: symbol, method: symbol)
.input process_register

// A process allocation site: the start at `id` starts `proc`. A spawn runs the function spawn_call resolved; a GenServer, :gen_server or :gen_statem start runs its literal callback module, as does a supervisor's start_child of a child spec naming one.
//
.decl process_start(id: symbol, func: symbol, proc: symbol, kind: symbol, runs: symbol)
.input process_start

// A call that dispatches through a protocol, so its target is whichever implementation the argument's type provides.
//
// Distinct from `unknown_call`: that one means the effect model has no entry, which somebody could add. This one means there is no single answer to have — any module may define an implementation, and it is ordinary code that can do anything.
//
.decl protocol_dispatch(id: symbol, caller: symbol, api: symbol)
.input protocol_dispatch

// A function its module declared free of observable effects with `@pure true` (see `Argus.Purity`).
//
// Read out of the beam's persisted attribute chunk, so the contract comes from the compiled artifact and cannot drift from the code it describes.
//
.decl pure_contract(func: symbol, mod: symbol, name: symbol, arity: number)
.input pure_contract

// What a receive matches, one row per clause: a literal atom, or 'any' for a clause whose pattern is not an atom (a tuple, a wildcard, a guard on the message). Says whether a flush after cancel_timer/1 takes the timer's own message.
//
.decl recv_pattern(id: symbol, func: symbol, message: symbol)
.input recv_pattern

// A field Ecto excludes from `inspect/1`. Absence is the interesting case — `redact` defaults to off — so consumers ask by negation.
//
.decl redacted_field(mod: symbol, field: symbol)
.input redacted_field

// `func` returns the result of a call to `callee`: a tail call, or a call followed by return. The chain `defp arm(ms), do: Process.send_after(...)` is one hop; default-argument wrappers add more.
//
.decl returns_call(func: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input returns_call

// What the function returns depends on the source: a lookup helper returns its site, a wrapper the call it makes, an identity function its parameter.
//
.decl returns_depends(func: symbol, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input returns_depends

// RPC call with timeout information.
.decl rpc_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, variant: symbol, timeout: symbol)
.input rpc_call

// How the result of an :rpc.call / :rpc.multicall / :erpc.call is treated: compared to :badrpc somewhere in the function; tested as a boolean (where a {:badrpc, _} tuple is truthy); matched by shape in a function with a clause-less exit (a CaseClauseError or MatchError on {:badrpc, _}); matched with a wildcard; returned as the function's own result; or stored or passed on unexamined.
//
.decl rpc_result(id: symbol, func: symbol, handling: symbol)
.input rpc_result

// A field on an Ecto schema, read from the literal in __schema__/1.
.decl schema_field(mod: symbol, field: symbol)
.input schema_field

// call_arg_derived at a sink site — atom creation, deserialization, code execution — keyed on the site because the finding anchors there. Together with call_arg_derived it lets a rule chain a request entry's parameter to the sink's argument: a proven flow rather than a call path.
//
.decl sink_arg_derived(id: symbol, func: symbol, arg_pos: number, param_pos: number)
.input sink_arg_derived

// The operation runs only because of a test on the source, or is handed a value made from it (Argus.Extractors.Dependence). A site source is another shared-state operation's result: the check a check-then-act acts on.
//
.decl site_depends(site: symbol, func: symbol, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input site_depends

// site_depends by data alone: the operation's arguments are made from the source, not merely computed under a test on it. An insert whose object carries what a lookup returned writes the read back; one that only runs because of the lookup writes something else.
//
.decl site_reads(site: symbol, func: symbol, kind: symbol, source: symbol)
.input site_reads

// A socket transport an endpoint enables, read from the literal that `socket/3` compiles into `__sockets__/0`. Emitted only for transports that are present and not `false`, which is how Phoenix reads them.
//
.decl socket_transport(endpoint: symbol, path: symbol, transport: symbol)
.input socket_transport

// What a function's `@spec` claims it returns, normalized (`Argus.Specs`): `can_fail` when the return type names {:error, _}, :error, nil, false, :undefined or {:EXIT, _}; `total` when it is known and names none of them; `constant` when it is one literal atom (and so also total); `no_return`; `returns_pid`. A function may have several shapes, and one with no row is unknown — no spec, a `term()` return, a module shipped without specs (:mnesia) — never "cannot fail". Rows come from the analyzed module's own beam for its functions, and from the code path for the remote functions it calls; clientlib/specs.dl prefers the first. A spec is an unverified claim: rules use these rows only to suppress or confirm a finding, never to report one on their own.
//
.decl spec_return(func: symbol, shape: symbol, origin: symbol)
.input spec_return

// The function compares against the atom somewhere — the loser of a start race is taken. Any comparison anywhere counts, so an unrelated one keeps the race rule quiet.
//
.decl start_error_compared(func: symbol, atom: symbol)
.input start_error_compared

// A {:call, from} clause returns without a reply action, without postponing, and without keeping `from`: the caller stays blocked. Pattern tests carry the previous clause's line, so `tag` is what a consumer with the source finds the clause head by.
.decl statem_call_unreplied(mod: symbol, func: symbol, site: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input statem_call_unreplied

// Some clause of the callback accepts any event: a body is reachable from the entry by failure branches alone.
.decl statem_event_catchall(mod: symbol, func: symbol)
.input statem_event_catchall

// A gen_statem callback has a clause for this event type. Over-approximated the same way callback_tag is — every comparison of the first argument counts — so consumers ask which types are NOT handled.
//
.decl statem_event_clause(mod: symbol, func: symbol, event_type: symbol)
.input statem_event_clause

// Some clause of the callback accepts an :info event with any content: from a test establishing the event type is :info, a body is reachable without passing the success branch of a test on any other register.
//
.decl statem_info_catchall(mod: symbol, func: symbol)
.input statem_info_catchall

// Some clause other than a catch-all takes the event content by its shape alone, never comparing it or its tag to a value — `callback_open` for a gen_statem's content, `{x, 1}`.
//
.decl statem_info_open(mod: symbol, func: symbol, shape: symbol)
.input statem_info_open

// An atom the callback compares somewhere — an event content's tag among the event types and state names. Over-approximated as `callback_tag` is: a rule asks whether a tag is NOT taken.
//
.decl statem_info_tag(mod: symbol, func: symbol, tag: symbol)
.input statem_info_tag

// Initial state declared by `init/1`'s `{:ok, State, Data}` return (one row per resolvable clause — a machine with multiple init clauses has several). Read directly from the return rather than inferred topologically, so the reachability analysis knows which no-incoming state is the legitimate entry point. Only literal-atom states are recorded; a computed initial state emits no row.
//
.decl statem_initial(mod: symbol, state: symbol)
.input statem_initial

// Module implementing gen_statem behaviour.
.decl statem_module(mod: symbol, callback_mode: symbol)
.input statem_module

// State in a gen_statem state machine.
.decl statem_state(mod: symbol, state: symbol, site: symbol)
.input statem_state

// Timeout set in a gen_statem state.
.decl statem_timeout(mod: symbol, state: symbol, type: symbol, value: symbol)
.input statem_timeout

// State transition in a gen_statem.
.decl statem_transition(mod: symbol, from_state: symbol, event: symbol, to_state: symbol)
.input statem_transition

// A synchronous management call into a supervisor process. Every one is a GenServer.call underneath — `start_child` waits for the child's init/1 to return, `terminate_child` for the child's whole shutdown — but none names a GenServer module, so `sync_call` never saw them. `target` is resolved from the first argument like `sync_call`'s callee.
//
.decl sup_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, op: symbol, target: symbol)
.input sup_call

// Module that implements the Supervisor behaviour.
.decl supervisor(mod: symbol, strategy: symbol)
.input supervisor

// Child specification within a supervisor.
.decl supervisor_child(sup: symbol, position: number, child_mod: symbol, restart: symbol, type: symbol)
.input supervisor_child

// Whether a child spec stated its `type`, or whether `supervisor_child.type` is the extractor's default.
//
// The `{Module, args}` and bare-`Module` forms state nothing: `Module.child_spec/1` decides, and `use Supervisor` generates `type: :supervisor` where the default written here is `worker`. Any rule reading `type` must join this, or it is reasoning about a guess — a first attempt at "supervisor registered as a worker" reported 26 modules on the corpus and every one was this artefact.
//
.decl supervisor_child_form(sup: symbol, position: number, form: symbol)
.input supervisor_child_form

// Registered name declared in a child spec's `:name` option — e.g. the `MyApp.Pool` in `{DynamicSupervisor, name: MyApp.Pool}`. Recorded alongside `supervisor_child` (same `sup`/`position`) so a `dynamic_child` whose parent is a registered name can be anchored to the child that registers it: a `DynamicSupervisor.start_child(MyApp.Pool, _)` call resolves to the named child instead of appearing unanchored. Only atom names are recorded — `{:via, _, _}` and `{:global, _}` names are not, since name-based `start_child` targets are always atoms.
//
.decl supervisor_child_name(sup: symbol, position: number, name: symbol)
.input supervisor_child_name

// A `max_children` cap read from `DynamicSupervisor.init/1`'s literal options. Present ONLY when a finite cap is set: the behaviour defaults to `:infinity`, so absence is the common case and the interesting one, and consumers ask about it by negation.
//
.decl supervisor_max_children(sup: symbol, limit: symbol)
.input supervisor_max_children

// Anchor site of a supervisor's tree definition — positional.
.decl supervisor_site(mod: symbol, site: symbol)
.input supervisor_site

// GenServer.call target detected in code.
.decl sync_call(caller_func: symbol, callee_mod: symbol)
.input sync_call

// A synchronous call's target and timeout at its site: what pairs a dependency with the timeout of the call that makes it, where sync_call_timeout says only that the function makes such a call.
//
.decl sync_call_site(id: symbol, caller_func: symbol, callee_mod: symbol, timeout_ms: number)
.input sync_call_site

// GenServer.call timeout value at call site.
.decl sync_call_timeout(caller_func: symbol, callee_mod: symbol, timeout_ms: number)
.input sync_call_timeout

// An ETS table allocation site, an object of the same points-to analysis a process is: the reference an unnamed table is, or the name a named table is answered with, flows from here as a `table` source through parameters, returns, fields and a server's state.
//
.decl table_alloc(id: symbol, func: symbol, table: symbol)
.input table_alloc

// The table operand of the ETS operation at `id` is the source: clientlib/tables.dl chains it to the tables it may be.
//
.decl table_use(id: symbol, func: symbol, src_kind: symbol, src: symbol)
.input table_use

// A timer armed at `id`: whether it targets the arming process, and whether its message is a literal that cannot be told from an earlier instance of itself ('bare'), one of the function's own parameters ('param', with its position, for a rule to resolve at the callers through resolved_arg), or a computed value such as a ref ('dynamic'). Refines the `timer` / `timer_bare` kinds of mailbox_writer.
//
.decl timer_arm(id: symbol, func: symbol, target: symbol, message: symbol, param: number, literal: symbol)
.input timer_arm

// Where the ref cancelled at `id` came from: a map field read in the function (`state.timer`, a `%{timer: ref}` head), one of the function's parameters (resolved at the callers through call_arg_field), the ref a send_after in the same function returned ('local', keyed by that site), or unknown.
//
.decl timer_cancel(id: symbol, func: symbol, source: symbol, key: symbol, param: number)
.input timer_cancel

// Where the ref of the timer armed at `id` goes: returned by the function (an arming helper), stored under a literal key of a map (`%{state | timer: ...}`, `Map.put(state, :timer, ...)`), or somewhere the walk cannot follow.
//
.decl timer_ref(id: symbol, func: symbol, flow: symbol, key: symbol)
.input timer_ref

// A map update in `func` stores the result of a call to `callee` under `key`: `%{state | timer: arm(ms)}`. With returns_call this ties a timer ref to the state field that keeps it.
//
.decl timer_store(func: symbol, key: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input timer_store

// A call establishing a TLS session. `dynamic` options are recorded as such rather than guessed at: a false "this is insecure" on a call that configures itself properly is worse than silence.
//
.decl tls_connect(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, opts: symbol)
.input tls_connect

// How a TLS session verifies its peer, read from literal option lists and bare atoms. `absent` means a connect supplied literal options that never mention `verify`, so the library's default applies — Erlang's `:ssl` client verified nothing at all before OTP 26.
//
.decl tls_verification(id: symbol, func: symbol, setting: symbol)
.input tls_verification

// Process.flag(:trap_exit, true) call site.
.decl trap_exit(func: symbol, mod: symbol)
.input trap_exit

// A peer call the try guards — GenServer.call, :gen_statem.call, :erpc.call and their kin — whose failure the handler is expected to classify. `call` is where a finding points: the try instruction carries the line of whatever preceded it.
//
.decl try_call(id: symbol, func: symbol, callee: symbol, call: symbol, guard_end: symbol)
.input try_call

// A call the effect model has no opinion about — neither known-impure nor known-pure.
//
// Recorded rather than ignored because purity is a claim about every execution. Assuming unknown calls are harmless would make a verification report success far more often and mean nothing.
//
.decl unknown_call(id: symbol, caller: symbol, api: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input unknown_call

// Unsafe atom creation from dynamic input.
.decl unsafe_atom_creation(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol)
.input unsafe_atom_creation

// Binary-to-term deserialization call with safety classification.
.decl unsafe_deserialization(id: symbol, func: symbol, api: symbol, safety: symbol)
.input unsafe_deserialization
